# {{.Name}}

A Go microservice following clean architecture principles.

## Project Structure

```
.
├── cmd/                    # Application entry points
│   └── main.go            # Main application with fx dependency injection
├── config/                 # Configuration files
│   ├── config.go          # Configuration struct and loader
│   └── config.yaml        # Default configuration
├── internal/
│   ├── domain/            # Business logic and entities
│   │   ├── entities       # Domain entities
│   │   ├── use cases      # Business logic orchestration
│   │   └── interfaces     # Repository and service contracts
│   ├── entrypoints/       # External interfaces
│   │   ├── http/          # HTTP handlers (Fiber)
│   │   └── messaging/     # Message consumers
│   └── drivenadapters/    # Infrastructure implementations
│       ├── database/      # Database adapters
│       ├── api/           # External API clients
│       ├── messaging/     # Message publishers
│       └── cache/         # Cache adapters
└── README.md
```

## Architecture Layers

### Domain Layer
Contains core business logic, entities, use cases, and interfaces. No external dependencies.

### EntryPoints Layer
Handles external requests (HTTP, message queues) and calls domain use cases.

### DrivenAdapters Layer
Implements domain interfaces for external dependencies (databases, APIs, caches).

## Dependency Injection with uber-go/fx

This project uses [uber-go/fx](https://uber-go.github.io/fx/) for dependency injection. Each generated component includes an fx Module that can be wired into the main application.

### Wiring Generated Modules

`cmd/main.go` contains two pairs of marker comments:

```go
// scaffold-go:imports:begin
// scaffold-go:imports:end
```

```go
// scaffold-go:modules:begin
// scaffold-go:modules:end
```

`scaffold-go generate ep consumer ...` and `scaffold-go generate da publisher ...`
**automatically** insert the right import and module reference between these
markers, so `cmd/main.go` is updated for you. Re-running the same generator is
idempotent. If you remove the markers, the CLI prints a warning and you'll need
to wire the new module by hand. The expected shape after wiring looks like:

```go
import (
    "{{.ModulePath}}/internal/entrypoints/messaging" // package consumers
    "{{.ModulePath}}/internal/drivenadapters/messaging" // package publishers
)
```

## Getting Started

```bash
# Install dependencies
go mod tidy

# Run the application
go run cmd/main.go

# Build
go build -o {{.Name}} cmd/main.go
```

## Generate Components

The recommended order when adding a new feature is:
**1.** entity → **2.** usecase → **3.** entry-point (`ep`) → **4.** driven-adapter (`da`).

### Domain Layer

```bash
# Generate entity with repository interface
scaffold-go generate entity User --fields Name:string,Email:string,Age:int

# Generate use case
scaffold-go generate usecase CreateUser --dependencies userRepo:UserRepository
```

### EntryPoints Layer (`ep`)

```bash
# Generate HTTP handler entry-point (Fiber)
scaffold-go generate ep http User --usecases createUser:CreateUserUseCase --routes POST:/users:CreateUser

# RabbitMQ consumer (event | command | query | notification)
scaffold-go generate ep consumer OrderEventConsumer \
  --broker rabbitmq --pattern event \
  --usecases processOrder:ProcessOrderUseCase \
  --queue order.created

# IBM MQ listener (or requestreply)
scaffold-go generate ep consumer InvoiceListener \
  --broker ibmmq --pattern listener \
  --queue DEV.QUEUE.INVOICES
```

### DrivenAdapters Layer (`da`)

```bash
# Generate database driven adapter (PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or DynamoDB)
scaffold-go generate da database UserRepo --type postgres --entity User --fields Name:string,Email:string

# Generate external API client
scaffold-go generate da api PaymentClient --url https://api.payment.com

# RabbitMQ publisher (event | command | query | notification)
scaffold-go generate da publisher OrderEventPublisher \
  --broker rabbitmq --pattern event \
  --target order.created

# IBM MQ sender (or requestreply)
scaffold-go generate da publisher InvoiceSender \
  --broker ibmmq --pattern sender \
  --target DEV.QUEUE.INVOICES

# Generate cache adapter
scaffold-go generate da cache UserCache
```

### Messaging brokers

`generate ep consumer` and `generate da publisher` are broker-aware and pull in
the matching client library:

| `--broker`  | Library                                                      |
|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
| `rabbitmq`  | [bancolombia/reactive-commons-go](https://github.com/bancolombia/reactive-commons-go) |
| `ibmmq`     | [bancolombia/commons-mq-go](https://github.com/bancolombia/commons-mq-go) (CGO + IBM MQ C client) |
| `kafka`     | placeholder; the CLI returns "not implemented yet"           |

The first generation per broker also emits a shared foundation module under
`internal/infrastructure/messaging/<broker>/` that exposes the singleton client
to all generated consumers and publishers. After running a messaging generator,
remember to run `go mod tidy` to fetch the broker dependency.

## Development Workflow

1. Generate domain entities and use cases
2. Generate entrypoints (handlers, consumers)
3. Generate driven adapters (database, API clients, cache)
4. Wire modules in `cmd/main.go`
5. Implement business logic in use cases
6. Run and test

## Testing

```bash
# Run all tests
go test ./...

# Run tests with coverage
go test -cover ./...

# Run specific package tests
go test ./internal/domain/...
```
